WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.830
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Timothy Morton, who's an ecological philosopher

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.440
at rice university, also

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.100
author of the book being ecological out with
penguin and MIT.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.550
It's so great to have you on today.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:15.519
Oh, thank you so much for having me.

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:19.029
It's an honor and if I couldn't do things
like this, I couldn't think you have to sort

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:21.440
of work with other people to be able to do
that.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.119
If you're, if you're me, let's

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:24.119
do it.

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:26.930
I mean, I think maybe to start, let's define
some terms a little bit.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:33.480
I think that maybe the term ecology and ecological
is not often understood by many.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.050
There's sort of something in there that relates
to the environment.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.670
There's something in there that relates to
climate change or maybe not.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:44.350
So can we maybe start with defining what the
field is and what we're talking about?

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:49.370
Well, the funny thing is that the word ecology
is like the word economy.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.700
And we sort of know what economy means.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:57.280
Um, economy means like how you organize your
house actually that first two syllables, the

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:05.410
eco bit means house in Greek, you know, incoming,
outgoing flows of money and whatever.

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:08.569
And roughly it's, it's sort of to do how you
organize your enjoyment, right?

00:01:08.569 --> 00:01:13.990
You know, like I'll give you 50 cents and
you give me this ice cream as a free cheap

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.109
ice cream for some reason.

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:19.869
Um, ecology is in a way the logic of that.

00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:22.210
It's the logic of where we live.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.929
Um, how it, how it works.

00:01:23.929 --> 00:01:28.329
And the funny thing is of course, is that
classical economic theory doesn't seem to

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:31.929
have anything to do very much with coral or
mountains or whatever.

00:01:31.929 --> 00:01:36.099
But I guess we're finding out right now just
how connected these things are.

00:01:36.099 --> 00:01:42.939
Um, that actually by ignoring, um, some of
the things like coral and mountains, um, we

00:01:42.939 --> 00:01:46.829
might have, uh, got some things a bit screwy
with, with our, with our economics, with our

00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:48.719
way of doing it.

00:01:48.719 --> 00:01:49.719
So

00:01:49.719 --> 00:01:54.029
what would be some examples of maybe the questions
that would be the questions that the field

00:01:54.029 --> 00:01:55.029
considers?

00:01:55.029 --> 00:02:03.409
Oh, well, um, one very strong question would
be, uh, at what scales is this thing happening?

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:04.409
Right?

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:08.270
Like in a funny way, ecological awareness
just means being aware that things are happening

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.569
on more than one scale at once.

00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:17.290
So for example, you start your car now that's
actually, you know, on a planet scale, statistically

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:20.770
meaningless, you haven't done anything, you
shouldn't feel built guilty in a way.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:25.400
Um, I like to say that maybe there's a difference
between guilty and responsible.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.760
Um, then billions and billions of those are
doing something.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.790
So there's a funny paradox, which is that
on one scale, nothing's happening.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.620
On another scale, everything is, is happening
and it's the same thing.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.810
You're doing the same thing.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:43.129
So in the, in the field of, you know, climate
change and environmentalism, one debate that

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:46.440
exists that I think is, uh, is a better one
than does it exist.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.030
I think that that's not a good debate at this
point.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.549
But one is personal responsibility versus
corporate responsibility.

00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:56.970
So the discussion of plastic straws and getting
a car with slightly better gas mileage or

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.739
whatever the case may be.

00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:03.620
And a counter to the entire framing of those
questions is that this is not really about

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:08.599
the decisions of the individual because the
overwhelming, I think it's, you know, 70%

00:03:08.599 --> 00:03:12.879
of emissions come from just a hundred companies
or something like that, so that the conversation

00:03:12.879 --> 00:03:18.379
is actually happening at the wrong scale within
the ecological framework.

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:22.939
As you say, you know, there's the little action
and things are happening on, on multiple layers.

00:03:22.939 --> 00:03:27.819
Is there a particular level of zooms, so to
speak, that is the predominant one in the

00:03:27.819 --> 00:03:29.330
questions you deal with?

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:36.310
Oh, well, um, you're quite right to suggest,
I feel that the, um, individual scale is a

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:40.249
bit too small and it also brings up questions
of good and evil.

00:03:40.249 --> 00:03:41.450
Like, am I doing this right?

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.939
You know, what's the right shampoo to choose?

00:03:43.939 --> 00:03:48.790
I've got 200 shampoos in the shop and I'm
confronted with them like this giant ice flow

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:53.170
and it's made of shampoo and I have to figure
out which one to do and it's quite alien,

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:54.450
you know?

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.819
Um, and um, of course there's a, there's an
awful feeling of powerlessness when you're

00:03:58.819 --> 00:04:01.090
thinking about something happening at planet
scale.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:05.790
And I'm old enough to remember those things
in the 90s where in the media sometimes people

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:10.719
would say things like, if we just did this
one thing like save toilet paper or jump up

00:04:10.719 --> 00:04:13.450
and down on the spot for awhile, then this
would happen.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:16.780
If we just did it for two weeks, then this
other amazing thing would happen.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.669
And it's as if the onus is put on, on us as
individuals.

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:24.460
And I, I like to distinguish between guilty
and responsible.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:28.350
I'm, I'm doing this interview across from
Chevron and all those companies cause I work

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:33.599
at rice university and in Houston and it's
quite clear who's guilty in a way.

00:04:33.599 --> 00:04:38.780
Like some people knew that global warming
was happening and that uh, and so forth quite

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:39.830
a long time ago.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:45.819
Um, rather like the cigarettes situation,
on the other hand, everyone's responsible

00:04:45.819 --> 00:04:49.520
and it doesn't mean that even that you have
to prove that you did it or do you have to

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.550
prove is that you can understand it, right?

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:56.349
Like if you see somebody running in the street
and they're going to get hit by a car, you're

00:04:56.349 --> 00:05:00.150
responsible for that person and you don't
have to prove even that you did it.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.900
Um, and even maybe it might waste some time.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.629
You know, you might be trying to prove that
you did it, the calculator and suddenly the

00:05:06.629 --> 00:05:07.810
person got hit anyway.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:13.199
So in a funny way, I feel like taking a lot
of that guilt of the people's shoulders and

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:19.620
I feel like people like me often make ordinary
people feel stupid and evil and, and that's

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.819
probably why our message isn't working so
great.

00:05:22.819 --> 00:05:28.660
What is it that when it comes to solving the
problems that we're dealing with, you see

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:33.560
as the biggest impediment because it's very
easy and I think at least partially accurate

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:39.449
to say that there are significant corporate
interests that stand to lose a lot of money

00:05:39.449 --> 00:05:43.050
if we actually start dealing seriously with
the problems that we're having.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:44.910
But that I think is only one piece of it.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:49.990
So are there others, you know, there's this
idea of analysis paralysis of endlessly seeking

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:51.379
facts without a path forward.

00:05:51.379 --> 00:05:52.580
I'll just open it.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:54.670
What else is preventing us from solving this?

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:59.919
Well, the way that we talk to each other about
it is quite inhibiting.

00:05:59.919 --> 00:06:05.319
Um, I'm sometimes like to think that it's
not exactly how, what you think that's could

00:06:05.319 --> 00:06:08.889
start world war four or whatever, but, but,
but how you think?

00:06:08.889 --> 00:06:09.889
Yeah.

00:06:09.889 --> 00:06:13.450
Like there are different ideas, for example,
about what the word belief means, you know,

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:14.450
disbelief.

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:15.729
I mean, hold on really, really tight or something.

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:16.979
I never let go.

00:06:16.979 --> 00:06:20.820
Or disbelieve means something more like trusting,
you know, just sort of allowing things to,

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.240
to, to be the case.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:27.560
And, um, so you know, in a way it, it's to
do with what we think all these deep things

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.720
about what does believe mean, what does responsible
mean?

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.910
And in general, how do we act in groups?

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:38.930
You know, I mean we're, we, we on our daily
lives, we are members of lots and lots of

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:42.170
overlapping intersecting different sizes of
group.

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:48.190
And if we're going to work with this global
warming stuff, it's a 7.5 billion group problem.

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:53.110
And, and that's a big challenge actually to
imagine ourselves as part of a group without

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:57.590
thinking that the group is like this glass
of water in which we just dissolve like salt

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.900
or sugar and we don't really mean anything
and we don't really exist.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.660
And it's a tremendously inhibiting to think,
you know, and again, perhaps it's a hangover

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:09.849
from not, not so great aspects of some religion
that's feeling like you're part of something

00:07:09.849 --> 00:07:10.849
bigger.

00:07:10.849 --> 00:07:13.039
It makes you feel like you don't count.

00:07:13.039 --> 00:07:14.039
Yeah.

00:07:14.039 --> 00:07:20.199
I, I in uh, of his book, guns, germs and steel,
Jared diamond talks quite a bit about the

00:07:20.199 --> 00:07:24.860
size of groups in which humans can function
in different ways.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:26.879
And to different degrees.

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:32.539
And it seems that when we're talking about
ecology, we, we really are talking about the

00:07:32.539 --> 00:07:33.539
planet.

00:07:33.539 --> 00:07:35.620
I mean everybody is part of it in a sense.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:38.610
And that seems overwhelming to some degree.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:39.949
Is this the role?

00:07:39.949 --> 00:07:41.419
Is this why we have governments?

00:07:41.419 --> 00:07:45.530
I mean, I don't, I don't want to be pessimistic
and maybe you're not pessimistic, but how

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:49.759
do we wrap our mind around how to solve a
problem of that scope and size?

00:07:49.759 --> 00:07:53.470
I have awful trouble with, with, um, dangle
two feelings about this.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:55.680
So I, I feel badly a lot.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.590
Um, so one of my favorite hobbies is feeling
badly.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:03.389
And, um, I feel like if I'm just lying there
in the fetal position, you know, on the floor,

00:08:03.389 --> 00:08:07.479
which is how I feel sometimes when I hear
this data, there's nothing I can do about

00:08:07.479 --> 00:08:08.479
it, you know?

00:08:08.479 --> 00:08:11.539
So I have to figure out how to scrape myself
off the floor and get up and do something.

00:08:11.539 --> 00:08:17.840
So one has to feel somewhat confident in the
face of the, the, the awfulness.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:25.771
Um, I think really that, um, what we're dealing
with is, uh, obviously a, again, it's a, it's

00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:32.200
a problem to do with, um, knowing that the,
that what you do at one scale might suck at

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:33.220
another scale, right?

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:34.770
Like, like very crudely.

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:39.940
Um, nothing that everything's sort of interconnected
on the planet means that if you're going to

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:43.890
be nice to bunny rabbits, it means that you're
not going to be nice to bunny rabbit parasites.

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:47.070
So part of it is this Groundhog day situation.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:51.170
If you remember the film Groundhog day, bill
Murray tries to get it right.

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:55.300
It keeps going through the same day over and
over again when he realizes he can never ever

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.980
save the guy who's going to get hit.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.200
And that's a little bit like ecological awareness.

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:05.430
There's always going to be some flaw in your
plan, but that doesn't mean you should, you

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:07.810
should not try to carry it out.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:12.750
And so part of it is, you know, realizing
that we're always going to feel a little bit

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:16.960
like Hippocrates and Murray's going to wonder
exactly why we're doing what we're doing.

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.700
They're on point enough reasons all the time.

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:25.530
When it comes to action, I mean, is there,
do you think on those terms, is there, when

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:32.720
we look at fossil fuels, trash, plastics,
the oceans, the ozone, all of these different,

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:37.580
is there even something that you would say
should be the areas of focus at this point?

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:43.360
Oh, well actually it's very easy for an individual
person, um, to realize that actually the best

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:46.680
thing you can do as an individual is to eat
a little bit less than need.

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:51.670
If you're wondering what to do right now and
you're feeling overwhelmed by all of this.

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:55.880
And the way we talk to each other about it
is so triggering and sort of PTSD, and you

00:09:55.880 --> 00:10:01.870
open the paper and it says that, you know,
500 to 16%, 20,000 years and it's also confusing.

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:06.330
And then there's another lot of data dumped
on either the next day, you know?

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:09.900
Um, and then you read the editorial section
and it's like, Oh, you're really evil.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:10.900
You're bad.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:11.900
You know?

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:14.430
And so no wonder people feel put off, but
actually there's something very simple you

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:15.670
can do.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:19.850
While we're waiting for people to do bigger
things, um, which is each a little bit less

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:20.850
meat.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:25.170
It's amazing how much fossil fuel emissions
there are in agriculture.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:29.140
And if you want to do something right now,
I would seriously advise, I would seriously

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:34.220
advise that as you've got a car and if you
do and it's probably quite expensive to pay

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:35.500
it off.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:38.830
Maybe it's better just to focus on the food
right now and not worry so much about about

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:40.490
the car aspect.

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:41.490
That's interesting.

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:45.430
And that's something I try to do and, and
I advocate for and yet if I recall correctly,

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:50.290
I think agricultural emissions or something
like 13% of total emissions.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:55.320
So we sort of are back in this position of
even if we all did it, we're dealing with

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:57.250
maybe a 10th of the problem.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:01.080
And I is, that may be what gets some people
to do nothing instead.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:05.650
I think so, yes, there's a feeling of paralysis
and you know, I guess one of the most important

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:11.430
things in the short term is to remember to
vote, you know, figure out who can represent

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:16.940
you, who can work some things out, um, politically
in, in, in larger groups.

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:20.370
Um, it's, it's probably more effective than
anything.

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:26.300
I think right now, even though some things
may seem more, um, to seem seemingly direct

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:31.520
and sort of more obvious or flamboyant, um,
for regular folks folks, I think just figuring

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:35.950
out who's the right person to vote for with
all of this is probably the best.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:38.390
It's probably the best tactic right now

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:43.230
in thinking a little more broadly, like maybe
five years, 10 years, 20 years are individual

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:47.810
conversations where you try to convince someone
who simply doesn't believe that we are affecting

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:49.350
the climate and our planet.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:54.320
Is that a worthwhile conversation to have
or are those sort of vestigial remains so

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:56.190
to speak, that will eventually go away?

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:59.230
And that's not really where our focus should
be arguing with people about whether it's

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:00.230
real.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:01.230
Gosh, that's interesting.

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:05.240
Um, well, you know, the funny thing is that,
you know, the wondering whether it's real

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:08.770
or not, is it a funny way, a symptom of the
fact that it's real?

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:12.070
I don't know if either there've been in a
car accident but, but, but, but when you're

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:17.840
in something like that, some kind of traumatic
situation, the thought is, gosh, is this really

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:18.840
happening?

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:22.580
And all around the world people are going,
is this actually for real, this global warming

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:24.360
mass extinction thing?

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:28.350
And that's actually a signal that it is happening
in a funny, in a funny way.

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:32.530
Another thing to say about this is that, um,
that kind of conversation where you try to

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:38.180
convince someone, yeah, probably that kind
of conversation has hoovered up as many people

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:40.890
as it possibly can now who want to believe
this stuff.

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:45.570
I think it's probably more effective to have
other kinds of conversation that don't necessarily

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:50.000
obviously direct, like for example, have a
conversation about responsibility.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:55.490
You know, like again, maybe jellyfish did
this, but because I can understand it, I can

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:56.810
responsible for it, right?

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:00.970
And because I've got fingers that can switch
off the keyboard switches that switch off

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:05.270
the oil pipes, I need to do this because dolphins
can't do it cause they flip.

00:13:05.270 --> 00:13:07.760
It's con like what the keyboards.

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:09.320
That's it.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:15.700
So, but I mean, so, so the, the idea and thinking
pragmatically then is that although there's

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:20.850
nothing wrong necessarily with trying to convince
people, but that has been going on for so

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:25.270
long that the convincible people likely already
have been, am I sort of understanding what

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:26.270
you're saying?

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:27.420
Yes, yes, exactly.

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:28.420
Exactly.

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:31.830
Is I, I feel like, you know, I, I was in the
local burger place.

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:34.870
I live in Texas and so water burger is our
Texas burger place.

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:36.820
We're all very proud of it.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:41.330
And, um, I just seen an incredible retired
by the adults are doing this incredible performance

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:43.360
of this Debussy ballet in Paris.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:47.180
And then the person who served me, the sources
cause as, suppose someone comes with a tray

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:51.510
with the sources or this place came to the
table to serve this.

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:56.310
I thought, gosh, your, you are handling yourself
as gracefully as this ballet dancer person

